<details class="card" <%= configured ? '' : 'open' %>>
  <summary><strong>How to get these credentials from Google Cloud</strong></summary>

  <p class="muted">One-time setup in Google Cloud. Mailsluice uses the Gmail API with a standard OAuth 2.0 Web-application flow -- no service account, no domain-wide delegation. Steps below match the Google Cloud console as of April 2026; if Google renames a page, the labels may drift.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Create / pick a project.</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/projectcreate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">console.cloud.google.com/projectcreate</a>. Make sure the project dropdown in the console title bar shows the one you want for the rest of the steps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Enable the Gmail API.</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/gmail.googleapis.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">API Library -&gt; Gmail API -&gt; Enable</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Branding (app info).</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/auth/branding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auth Platform -&gt; Branding</a>. Fill the app name, user support email, and developer contact email. The app logo is optional.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Audience.</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/auth/audience" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auth Platform -&gt; Audience</a>:
      <ul>
        <li>User type: <strong>External</strong>.</li>
        <li>Publishing status: keep it in <strong>Testing</strong> (caps you at 100 test users without Google's review -- plenty for self-hosting).</li>
        <li>Test users: add every Google account that will connect through this Mailsluice deployment (yourself at minimum). Anyone not on this list will get an <em>"access blocked"</em> error when they try to connect.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data Access (scopes).</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/auth/scopes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auth Platform -&gt; Data Access</a>. Add these three scopes (they are what Mailsluice requests):
      <ul>
        <li><code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify</code></li>
        <li><code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.labels</code></li>
        <li><code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/userinfo.email</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted"><code>gmail.modify</code> and <code>gmail.labels</code> are sensitive scopes -- that is expected and does not require Google verification while the app stays in Testing mode.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Create the OAuth client.</strong>
      <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/auth/clients" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auth Platform -&gt; Clients -&gt; + Create client</a>:
      <ul>
        <li><em>Application type:</em> <strong>Web application</strong></li>
        <li><em>Name:</em> anything, e.g. <code>Mailsluice</code></li>
        <li>
          <em>Authorized JavaScript origins:</em>
          <div class="card" style="margin:0.5rem 0; padding:0.75rem;">
            <code><%= suggested_origin %></code>
          </div>
          <p class="muted">If you will also access this deployment through another URL (e.g. behind a reverse proxy on a public hostname as well as via localhost), add each origin on its own line. Google matches these exactly.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>Authorized redirect URIs:</em>
          <div class="card" style="margin:0.5rem 0; padding:0.75rem;">
            <code><%= suggested_redirect_uri %></code>
          </div>
          <p class="muted">Add one redirect URI per environment (localhost for dev + your public URL for prod). One OAuth client holds many. The URI must match exactly -- trailing slash included.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Copy the client ID and client secret</strong> Google shows you after creating the client, and paste them into the form below. If you closed that dialog, you can reopen it from the Clients list (three-dot menu -&gt; <em>Download JSON</em>, or reveal the secret).
    </li>
  </ol>
</details>

<% if (configured) { %>
  <div class="card">
    <div><strong>Currently configured.</strong></div>
    <div class="muted">client_id: <%= client_id %></div>
    <div class="muted">client_secret: <%= client_secret_mask %></div>
    <% if (redirect_uri) { %>
      <div class="muted">redirect_uri: <%= redirect_uri %></div>
    <% } else { %>
      <div class="muted">redirect_uri: (default) <%= suggested_redirect_uri %></div>
    <% } %>
  </div>
<% } %>

<h3><%= configured ? 'Replace credentials' : 'Set credentials' %></h3>
<form class="stack" method="POST" action="/admin/destinations/gmail">
  <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">
  <label>
    client_id
    <input type="text" name="client_id" class="wide" value="<%= client_id %>" required autocomplete="off" placeholder="XXXXXXXXXXXX.apps.googleusercontent.com">
  </label>
  <label>
    client_secret
    <input type="password" name="client_secret" class="wide" <%= configured ? '' : 'required' %> autocomplete="new-password" placeholder="<%= configured ? 'leave blank to keep current secret' : 'GOCSPX-...' %>">
  </label>
  <label>
    redirect_uri (optional override)
    <input type="text" name="redirect_uri" class="wide" value="<%= redirect_uri %>" autocomplete="off" placeholder="<%= suggested_redirect_uri %>">
    <span class="muted">Leave blank to use <code><%= suggested_redirect_uri %></code> (derived from this request). Set it only if you have multiple redirect URIs in Google and want to force a specific one.</span>
  </label>
  <button type="submit">Save</button>
</form>

<% if (configured) { %>
  <h3>Clear</h3>
  <form method="POST" action="/admin/destinations/gmail/clear">
    <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">
    <button type="submit" class="danger">Clear Gmail OAuth client</button>
    <p class="muted">Clearing removes the credentials from the database. Existing user-level tokens are not revoked; users can disconnect their Gmail individually.</p>
  </form>
<% } %>
